.SS Abstract
Declares a function parameter list that can accept \f[B]any number of
arguments of any type\f[R], in a way that keeps working across C
standards.
.PP
Before C23, an empty parameter list \f[CR]()\f[R] meant \(dqunspecified
arguments\(dq (a non\-prototype declaration), so \f[CR]int f();\f[R]
could be called with anything.
\f[B]C23 changed this\f[R]: \f[CR]()\f[R] now means exactly the same as
\f[CR](void)\f[R], which breaks code that relied on the old behavior.
At the same time, C23 started allowing a variadic list with no named
parameter before it, \f[CR](...)\f[R], which C++ has always allowed.
.PP
\f[CR]MAYBE_ARGS\f[R] picks the right form for the current compiler:
.PP
