.PP
In the pre\-C23 case, the header also silences the
\f[CR]\-Wstrict\-prototypes\f[R] (GCC / Clang) and
\f[CR]\-Wdeprecated\-non\-prototype\f[R] (Clang) warnings that
\f[CR]f()\f[R] would otherwise produce.
.SS Content
.IP
.EX
#define MAYBE_ARGS
#define maybe_args
.EE
.PP
\f[B]Examples\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
\f[I]// A generic function pointer type that can hold \(dqany\(dq callback\f[R]
\f[B]typedef\f[R] void    (*t_callback)(MAYBE_ARGS);

\f[I]// A declaration that accepts any arguments on every standard\f[R]
\f[B]extern\f[R] int  legacy_handler(maybe_args);
.EE
.PP
Note
.PP
The warning suppression uses \f[CR]#pragma ... diagnostic ignored\f[R]
without a push/pop pair, so on pre\-C23 C it stays in effect for the
rest of the translation unit after this header is included.
.PP
Warning
.PP
\f[CR]f(...)\f[R] and \f[CR]f()\f[R] are not identical at the ABI level.
Calling a non\-variadic function through a \f[CR](...)\f[R] pointer (C23
/ C++) is undefined behavior; cast the pointer back to its real type
before calling it.
